**Pattern 1: CQRS without event sourcing — same database, different models**

Simple case: one DB, two sets of classes. Commands update normalized tables; queries read denormalized views (materialized views or hand-maintained).

```java
// Write side — normalized aggregate
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id String id;
    String customerId;
    @OneToMany List<OrderLine> lines;
    OrderStatus status;
    BigDecimal total;

    public void place() {
        if (status != DRAFT) throw new IllegalStateException();
        if (lines.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException();
        this.status = PLACED;
        DomainEvents.publish(new OrderPlaced(this.id));
    }
}

@Service
public class PlaceOrderCommandHandler {
    @Transactional
    public void handle(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order o = orderRepo.findById(cmd.orderId).orElseThrow();
        o.place();
        orderRepo.save(o);
    }
}

// Read side — denormalized view
@Entity
@Table(name = "order_summary_view")
public class OrderSummary {
    @Id String orderId;
    String customerName;
    String customerEmail;
    String productSummary;    // "Widget x2, Gadget x1"
    BigDecimal total;
    String status;
    Instant placedAt;
}

@Service
public class OrderQueryService {
    public List<OrderSummary> findByCustomer(String customerId) {
        return orderSummaryRepo.findByCustomerIdOrderByPlacedAtDesc(customerId);
    }
}
```

Maintain `order_summary_view` via:
- Materialized view refreshed periodically
- Triggers on write tables
- Application-level update after command
- CDC (Debezium reading binlog) feeding projection

**Pattern 2: CQRS with event sourcing**

Write side stores events, not state. Read side builds projections from event stream.

```java
// Write side — event-sourced aggregate
public class Order {
    String id;
    OrderStatus status;
    List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();
    int version = 0;
    List<Object> uncommittedEvents = new ArrayList<>();

    public static Order create(String id, String customerId) {
        Order o = new Order();
        o.apply(new OrderCreated(id, customerId, Instant.now()));
        return o;
    }

    public void addLine(String productId, int qty, BigDecimal price) {
        if (status != DRAFT) throw new IllegalStateException();
        apply(new LineAdded(id, productId, qty, price));
    }

    public void place() {
        if (status != DRAFT || lines.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException();
        apply(new OrderPlaced(id, Instant.now()));
    }

    private void apply(Object event) {
        uncommittedEvents.add(event);
        mutate(event);
    }

    private void mutate(Object event) {
        if (event instanceof OrderCreated e) { this.id = e.id; this.status = DRAFT; }
        else if (event instanceof LineAdded e) { lines.add(new OrderLine(e.productId, e.qty, e.price)); }
        else if (event instanceof OrderPlaced e) { this.status = PLACED; }
        version++;
    }

    public static Order rebuild(List<Object> events) {
        Order o = new Order();
        events.forEach(o::mutate);
        return o;
    }
}

// Event store
@Service
public class OrderCommandHandler {
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        List<Object> existing = eventStore.readEvents(cmd.orderId);
        Order order = existing.isEmpty() ? Order.create(cmd.orderId, cmd.customerId)
                                          : Order.rebuild(existing);
        cmd.lines.forEach(l -> order.addLine(l.productId, l.qty, l.price));
        order.place();
        eventStore.append(cmd.orderId, order.getUncommittedEvents(), order.version);
        order.getUncommittedEvents().forEach(eventBus::publish);
    }
}

// Read side — projection
@Component
public class OrderSummaryProjector {
    @EventListener
    public void on(OrderCreated e) {
        summaryRepo.save(new OrderSummary(e.id, e.customerId, "DRAFT"));
    }

    @EventListener
    public void on(LineAdded e) {
        OrderSummary s = summaryRepo.findById(e.orderId).orElseThrow();
        s.addProductSummary(e.productId, e.qty);
        summaryRepo.save(s);
    }

    @EventListener
    public void on(OrderPlaced e) {
        OrderSummary s = summaryRepo.findById(e.orderId).orElseThrow();
        s.markPlaced(e.placedAt);
        summaryRepo.save(s);
    }
}
```

Now the read model is entirely derived from events. If you want a new read shape, write a new projector that replays all events. Powerful for adding views later without touching write model.

**Pattern 3: Same DB, different views**

Not every CQRS needs separate DBs. Postgres materialized views or denormalized tables updated by triggers are valid.

```sql
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW order_summary AS
SELECT o.id, o.customer_id, c.name AS customer_name, 
       string_agg(p.name || ' x' || ol.qty, ', ') AS products,
       o.total, o.status, o.created_at
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id
JOIN order_lines ol ON ol.order_id = o.id
JOIN products p ON p.id = ol.product_id
GROUP BY o.id, c.name;

REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY order_summary;
```

Simple and effective when DB tech doesn't need to differ.

The aggregate is a plain Java class that captures business invariants and produces events. There are no JPA annotations and no setters that the framework calls — every state change goes through `apply(event)`.

```java
public class OrderAggregate {
    private String id;
    private OrderStatus status;
    private final List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();
    private int version = 0;
    private final List<DomainEvent> uncommitted = new ArrayList<>();

    public static OrderAggregate create(String id, String customerId) {
        OrderAggregate o = new OrderAggregate();
        o.apply(new OrderCreated(id, customerId, Instant.now()));
        return o;
    }

    public static OrderAggregate replay(List<DomainEvent> history) {
        OrderAggregate o = new OrderAggregate();
        history.forEach(o::mutate);
        return o;
    }

    public void place() {
        if (status != OrderStatus.DRAFT)
            throw new IllegalStateException("already placed");
        if (lines.isEmpty())
            throw new IllegalStateException("no items");
        apply(new OrderPlaced(id, Instant.now()));
    }

    private void apply(DomainEvent e) { uncommitted.add(e); mutate(e); }

    private void mutate(DomainEvent e) {
        switch (e) {
            case OrderCreated c -> { id = c.orderId(); status = OrderStatus.DRAFT; }
            case LineAdded a   -> lines.add(new OrderLine(a.productId(), a.qty(), a.price()));
            case OrderPlaced p -> status = OrderStatus.PLACED;
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("unknown event " + e);
        }
        version++;
    }

    public List<DomainEvent> uncommitted() { return List.copyOf(uncommitted); }
    public int version() { return version; }
}
```

The event store is a single append-only table. The command handler loads events for the aggregate, dispatches the command, and appends new events with optimistic concurrency on `(aggregate_id, version)`.

```java
@Service
public class PlaceOrderHandler {
    private final EventStore store;
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher bus;

    @Transactional
    public void handle(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        List<DomainEvent> history = store.read(cmd.orderId());
        OrderAggregate agg = history.isEmpty()
            ? OrderAggregate.create(cmd.orderId(), cmd.customerId())
            : OrderAggregate.replay(history);
        cmd.lines().forEach(l -> agg.addLine(l.productId(), l.qty(), l.price()));
        agg.place();
        // Optimistic-concurrency append; throws if version moved.
        store.append(cmd.orderId(), history.size(), agg.uncommitted());
        agg.uncommitted().forEach(bus::publishEvent);
    }
}
```

Projectors run on the read side. To survive consumer restarts and to fan out to other services, the events should be relayed to a message broker — the outbox pattern keeps the relay atomic with the event-store append because both are in the same DB transaction.

```java
@Entity @Table(name = "order_events_outbox")
public class OutboxRecord {
    @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
    String aggregateId;
    String eventType;
    @Column(columnDefinition = "jsonb") String payload;
    Instant createdAt = Instant.now();
    Instant publishedAt;
}

// Same DB transaction as event-store append — see PlaceOrderHandler.
@Component
public class OutboxAppender {
    private final OutboxRepository repo;
    private final ObjectMapper mapper;

    @EventListener
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.MANDATORY)
    public void on(DomainEvent e) throws IOException {
        OutboxRecord r = new OutboxRecord();
        r.aggregateId = e.aggregateId();
        r.eventType = e.getClass().getSimpleName();
        r.payload = mapper.writeValueAsString(e);
        repo.save(r);
    }
}

// Local in-process projector (read-your-writes side, transactional with the write).
@Component
public class OrderSummaryProjector {
    private final OrderSummaryRepository summaries;

    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void on(OrderCreated e) {
        summaries.save(new OrderSummary(e.orderId(), e.customerId(), "DRAFT"));
    }

    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void on(OrderPlaced e) {
        summaries.findById(e.orderId()).ifPresent(s -> {
            s.markPlaced(e.placedAt());
            summaries.save(s);
        });
    }
}

// Cross-service projectors run as Kafka consumers reading the relayed events.
@Component
public class SearchIndexProjector {
    private final SearchClient search;

    @KafkaListener(topics = "order-events", groupId = "search-projector")
    public void on(DomainEvent e) {
        // Idempotent upsert keyed by event id so replays are safe.
        search.upsert(e);
    }
}
```

The outbox row and the event-store append commit together; a separate poller (or Debezium reading the WAL) publishes the outbox to Kafka. Cross-service projectors consume from Kafka and update their own read stores. Local in-process projectors using `@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)` give the write side near-instant read-your-writes consistency for the same service's UI.
